@page "/docs/accessibility/audit"

@* izzy audit <url>: one page checked with axe-core and izzy's own checks in the machine's own browser; --run; what an account
   adds; every option; several pages in one run (--site, --urls; Pro) (Audit.cs, AuditSite.cs, Cli.cs). The first of the audit's
   six pages: verdicts, reports, fixes, builds and checks have their own. Until Nov 26, 2026 the audit was one article at
   docs/cli/audit (redirected here). *@

<ArticlePage Section="accessibility" Slug="audit">
    <Lead>
        <Code>izzy audit</Code> checks a page's accessibility with axe-core and checks of its own, in the Chrome or
        Edge already on your machine. It writes a report you can read and the same results as data. It reports
        and changes no code.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Alert Status="Status.Warning" Title="No findings is not conformance">
            A tool finds part of what WCAG asks. The rest needs a person with a keyboard and a screen reader.
            <Anchor Href="docs/accessibility/automated-and-manual">Automated and manual checks</Anchor> says which is which.
        </Alert>

        <Heading Level="2">Audit a page</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>izzy audit</Code> is a command of the <Anchor Href="docs/tools/cli">izzy CLI</Anchor>. Give it the
            page's address. The app can be running anywhere the browser can reach:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="bash">izzy audit https://localhost:5001/orders</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            To have izzy start the app in your project, audit it and stop it, add <Code>--run</Code> and give the
            page's path. Without a path it audits the home page:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="bash">izzy audit /orders --run</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            izzy opens the page five times: at 1440 and 375 pixels wide, in light and in dark, and once in forced
            colors. Some problems show at one width or in one scheme only. An element found more than once counts
            once.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            The terminal lists each rule that failed with its count of elements, and izzy writes its files to a new
            folder in <Code>.izzy/audit/</Code>. <Anchor Href="docs/accessibility/audit-reports">Reports</Anchor> lists
            them.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">What is free</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Auditing one page is free. What you get depends on whether izzy is signed in:
        </TextBlock>
        <Table Items="Tiers" Caption="What an audit gives, by account" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params" Bordered="false">
            <Columns>
                <TemplateColumn Title="With" Width="Length.Rem(14)">@context!.Name</TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="You get">@context!.Text</TemplateColumn>
            </Columns>
        </Table>
        <TextBlock>
            Sign in with <Code>izzy login</Code>: see <Anchor Href="docs/tools/account">Your account</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Pages behind a sign-in</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Sign in to your app in a browser, copy the session cookie's name and value from its developer tools, and
            pass it with <Code>--cookie</Code>. izzy sends it with every request. Repeat the option for more cookies:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="bash">izzy audit /orders --run --cookie .AspNetCore.Identity.Application=CfDJ8...</Code>

        <Heading Level="2">Audit the whole site</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            With IzzyBlazor Pro, <Code>--site</Code> audits every page of a site. izzy takes the pages from the site's
            <Code>sitemap.xml</Code>. Without one, it starts at the address you give and follows the links to the
            site's other pages. With <Code>--run</Code>, it audits the whole app and needs no address:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="bash">izzy audit --run --site</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            An address with a path keeps the audit inside it: <Code>izzy audit --site https://localhost:5001/docs</Code>
            audits <Code>/docs</Code> and the pages under it. A page is a path, so two addresses that differ only
            after the <Code>?</Code> are audited once. izzy stops at 500 pages; change that with
            <Code>--max-pages</Code>. A long run uses up to three browsers side by side.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            To audit pages of your own choosing, list their addresses in a file, one a line, and pass it with
            <Code>--urls</Code>:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="bash">izzy audit --urls pages.txt --run</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            Following links finds only what a link in the page's HTML leads to. A page reached another way, such
            as from a menu drawn by script, needs a sitemap or a line in the file.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Options</Heading>
        <Table Items="Options" Caption="Options of izzy audit" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params" Bordered="false">
            <Columns>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Option" Width="Length.Rem(14)"><code>@context!.Name</code></TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="What it does">@context!.Text</TemplateColumn>
            </Columns>
        </Table>

        <Heading Level="2">Next steps</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/accessibility/audit-verdicts">Verdicts</Anchor>: what each finding's verdict means, and how to accept one.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/accessibility/audit-reports">Reports</Anchor>: the files an audit writes.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/accessibility/audit-fixes">Find and check a fix</Anchor>: a finding's place in your code, and a recheck after you change it.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/accessibility/audit-builds">Audit in a build</Anchor>: fail a build on findings.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/accessibility/audit-checks">What the audit checks</Anchor>: each check, and what no run checks.</ListItem>
        </List>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>

@code {
    private sealed record Row(string Name, string Text);

    private static readonly Row[] Tiers =
    [
        new("No account", "axe-core and izzy's own checks on one page. The findings are listed as the tools gave them, and a finding of izzy's own checks has izzy's note. report.md and results.json."),
        new("A free account", "Also a verdict and its reason for each finding, the component that drew the element, the fix when izzy knows one and its line in your code. A task list, a report to hand over, a file for code scanning, a baseline to compare with, and the two tools for a coding agent."),
        new("IzzyBlazor Pro", "Also several pages in one run: a whole site with --site, or the pages you list with --urls."),
    ];

    private static readonly Row[] Options =
    [
        new("--run", "Starts the app in this project, or the one --project names, and stops it after the audit. With an account, findings are traced into its source."),
        new("--site", "Audits every page of the site: its sitemap's, else those its links lead to. Pro."),
        new("--urls <file>", "Audits the addresses a file lists, one a line. Pro: without it, izzy audits the first and says so."),
        new("--max-pages 500", "The most pages --site audits."),
        new("--cookie name=value", "Sends a cookie with every request, for pages behind a sign-in. Repeat it for more cookies."),
        new("--sizes 1440,375", "The widths to check, in pixels."),
        new("--schemes light,dark", "The color schemes to check. Each page is also opened once in forced colors."),
        new("--out <folder>", "Where the files go, in place of a new folder in .izzy/audit/."),
        new("--format md,json", "Which files to write. With an account, tasks adds tasks.md, html adds report.html and sarif adds results.sarif."),
        new("--project <path>", "The app's project: where --run starts it, and with an account where a finding's file and line are looked for."),
        new("--json", "Prints the results as JSON and writes no files."),
        new("--fail-on any", "Ends with exit code 5 when there are findings. With an account, real counts real findings and new counts real findings the baseline lacks."),
        new("--baseline <file>", "A past run's results.json to compare with. Needs an account."),
        new("--recheck <id>", "Audits again the page a past run's finding is on, and says whether it is fixed."),
        new("--browser <path>", "The Chrome, Edge or Chromium to use."),
    ];
}
